course: dictionary
lesson: 8832
title: Shine: light, bright, and skill
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Shine is a word with several meanings, so use the context to pick the right one. A thing can shine because it makes its own light, like the Sun. It can also shine because light bounces off it, like polished shoes. «The Sun shines because tiny parts of hydrogen join together deep inside it.» Here the Sun makes its own light.
explain: «Reflective strips shine the headlights straight back to the driver.» The strips do not make light. They send the car's light back, so they shine. Ask yourself: does the thing make its own light, or does it bounce light back? That question solves most shine puzzles.
explain: Shine can also be a job you do with light. To shine a torch means to point it at something so you can see it. Shine can also mean being very good at something. If someone shines at maths, they are excellent at it. The context always tells you which meaning is correct.
explain: Shine can be a noun too. It means the brightness of a polished surface, as in the shine on a new car. Geologists use shine to tell minerals apart, just as detectives look for clues. Read the words around shine, and you will know which meaning it has.
fact: Shine is a verb (shine, shines, shone or shined, shining) and also a noun, meaning brightness.
fact: The Sun and stars shine with their own light, but the Moon does not: it reflects sunlight.
fact: Shiny materials reflect light well, so they look bright.
fact: Never shine a torch or laser into someone's eyes, and never look straight at the Sun.
fact: Phrases: «rise and shine» means wake up, and «rain or shine» means whatever the weather.
exercise: «The Sun shines because tiny parts of hydrogen join together deep inside it.» In this sentence, shine means | To look bright because light bounces off it | To point a light at something | To be very good at something | *To give out its own bright light
exercise: Mia polished her shoes until they shone. What does shone mean here? | Was very good at polishing | Gave out its own light, like a star | *Looked bright because light reflected off them | Pointed a torch at them
exercise: «Reflective clothes shine in headlights at night.» What does shine mean here? | *Reflect bright light back | Disappear into the dark | Fall slowly to the ground | Make a loud noise
test: The Moon does not shine with its own light. What does it do instead? | Burns hydrogen deep inside | Turns the windscreen wipers on | *Reflects sunlight | Goes dark at night
test: Dad says, «Rise and shine!» What does he mean? | Look very bright and shiny | Go to sleep early | Point a torch at the wall | *Wake up
exam: Which action is safe? | Stare straight at the Sun to check the weather | *Wear reflective clothes when walking in the dark | Shine a torch straight into someone's eyes to say hello | Shine a laser at a friend to make them laugh
